1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,269
Seven o'clock on a Sunday morning.

2
00:00:02,269 --> 00:00:04,938
On a hilltop on the north shore of Oahu,

3
00:00:04,938 --> 00:00:09,476
two young Army privates are watching a radar screen that is supposed

4
00:00:09,476 --> 00:00:10,744
to be switched off.

5
00:00:10,744 --> 00:00:14,682
Then the biggest blip either of them has ever seen appears.

6
00:00:14,682 --> 00:00:20,354
Something enormous, a hundred and thirty-seven miles to the north, and coming closer.

7
00:00:20,354 --> 00:00:21,488
They phone it in.

8
00:00:21,488 --> 00:00:26,227
The officer on duty listens, and says five words: don't worry about it.

9
00:00:26,227 --> 00:00:30,097
Fifty minutes later, the first bombs fall on Pearl Harbor.

10
00:00:30,097 --> 00:00:32,500
That blip was not the first warning.

11
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:34,235
It was the last of dozens,

12
00:00:34,235 --> 00:00:38,172
spread over almost a year, read by the most powerful men in

13
00:00:38,172 --> 00:00:41,175
Washington, and every one of them was missed.

14
00:00:41,775 --> 00:00:43,939
Good evening, this is Brinkman.

15
00:00:43,939 --> 00:00:46,033
Every war starts at the brink,

16
00:00:46,033 --> 00:00:48,477
and tonight's brink is a countdown.

17
00:00:48,477 --> 00:00:53,154
Twelve days in late 1941, when the United States could read Japan's

18
00:00:53,154 --> 00:00:56,714
secret messages, sent its commanders a war warning,

19
00:00:56,714 --> 00:00:58,669
and still got caught asleep.

20
00:00:58,669 --> 00:01:01,322
This is not a story about one mistake.

21
00:01:01,322 --> 00:01:03,276
It is a story about signals,

22
00:01:03,276 --> 00:01:07,953
buried in noise, passed between people who never put them together.

23
00:01:07,953 --> 00:01:11,025
So let's open the file, and start the clock.

24
00:01:11,625 --> 00:01:14,699
To understand the attack, start with oil.

25
00:01:14,699 --> 00:01:18,747
By 1941 Japan had been at war in China for four years,

26
00:01:18,747 --> 00:01:24,070
and it imported about eighty percent of its oil from the United States.

27
00:01:24,070 --> 00:01:29,243
In July 1941, Japanese troops marched into southern French Indochina,

28
00:01:29,243 --> 00:01:34,715
putting bombers within reach of British Malaya and the Dutch East Indies.

29
00:01:34,715 --> 00:01:38,314
Washington answered by freezing Japanese assets.

30
00:01:38,314 --> 00:01:41,762
Britain and the Dutch followed, and by August,

31
00:01:41,762 --> 00:01:44,911
American oil had stopped flowing to Japan.

32
00:01:44,911 --> 00:01:49,109
Tokyo's navy had perhaps two years of fuel in its tanks.

33
00:01:49,109 --> 00:01:53,083
Its leaders saw only two ways out: give up the war in

34
00:01:53,083 --> 00:01:55,781
China, which the army refused to do,

35
00:01:55,781 --> 00:02:00,130
or seize the oil fields of the Dutch East Indies by force.

36
00:02:00,130 --> 00:02:04,178
And between Japan and that oil sat the American fleet,

37
00:02:04,178 --> 00:02:10,925
which President Roosevelt had moved from California to Pearl Harbor in 1940, as a warning.

38
00:02:11,525 --> 00:02:16,000
The man who planned the answer was Admiral Isoroku Yamamoto,

39
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:18,685
commander of Japan's Combined Fleet.

40
00:02:18,685 --> 00:02:22,488
He had studied at Harvard and served in Washington.

41
00:02:22,488 --> 00:02:25,919
He had seen American factories and oil fields,

42
00:02:25,919 --> 00:02:30,394
and he believed Japan could not win a long war against them.

43
00:02:30,394 --> 00:02:34,869
So he proposed a gamble: destroy the US Pacific Fleet at its

44
00:02:34,869 --> 00:02:38,001
anchorage in one blow, and buy six months,

45
00:02:38,001 --> 00:02:40,761
maybe a year, to take Southeast Asia.

46
00:02:40,761 --> 00:02:44,937
In November 1940 the British had shown it could be done,

47
00:02:44,937 --> 00:02:52,022
when old biplanes from one carrier crippled three Italian battleships in the harbor of Taranto.

48
00:02:52,022 --> 00:02:55,304
Pearl Harbor was only about forty feet deep,

49
00:02:55,304 --> 00:02:57,840
too shallow, the US Navy believed,

50
00:02:57,840 --> 00:03:00,450
for torpedoes dropped from the air.

51
00:03:00,450 --> 00:03:03,955
Japanese engineers fixed that with wooden fins.

52
00:03:03,955 --> 00:03:09,325
Pilots practiced low runs over a bay in southern Japan, again and again.

53
00:03:09,925 --> 00:03:11,470
And here is the twist.

54
00:03:11,470 --> 00:03:15,755
While Japan prepared, the United States was reading its mail.

55
00:03:15,755 --> 00:03:20,180
In 1940 a team of Army codebreakers led by William Friedman and

56
00:03:20,180 --> 00:03:25,730
Frank Rowlett cracked the machine cipher that Japan's Foreign Ministry used for

57
00:03:25,730 --> 00:03:28,118
its most secret diplomatic cables.

58
00:03:28,118 --> 00:03:30,295
The Americans called it Purple.

59
00:03:30,295 --> 00:03:34,931
They built copies of the Japanese machine without ever seeing one,

60
00:03:34,931 --> 00:03:39,216
and the intelligence it produced was given a codename: Magic.

61
00:03:39,216 --> 00:03:40,691
But Magic had limits.

62
00:03:40,691 --> 00:03:42,939
It read diplomats, not admirals.

63
00:03:42,939 --> 00:03:46,522
The navy's operational code was only partly broken,

64
00:03:46,522 --> 00:03:51,579
and almost nothing about the fleet's plans went out by diplomatic cable.

65
00:03:51,579 --> 00:03:55,372
Magic went to fewer than a dozen people in Washington.

66
00:03:55,372 --> 00:03:59,236
Purple machines went to the Philippines and to Britain.

67
00:03:59,236 --> 00:04:01,975
The commanders in Hawaii never got one.

68
00:04:02,575 --> 00:04:04,843
The first warnings came early.

69
00:04:04,843 --> 00:04:08,623
In January 1941, the American ambassador in Tokyo,

70
00:04:08,623 --> 00:04:14,368
Joseph Grew, cabled Washington that the Peruvian minister had heard a rumor:

71
00:04:14,368 --> 00:04:17,543
in case of trouble with the United States,

72
00:04:17,543 --> 00:04:21,172
Japan planned a surprise attack on Pearl Harbor.

73
00:04:21,172 --> 00:04:24,347
Naval intelligence judged it not credible.

74
00:04:24,347 --> 00:04:30,092
Then, in September, Tokyo sent its consulate in Honolulu an unusual request.

75
00:04:30,092 --> 00:04:33,872
Divide the waters of Pearl Harbor into five areas,

76
00:04:33,872 --> 00:04:38,181
and report exactly which warships are moored in each one.

77
00:04:38,181 --> 00:04:43,472
The consulate's spy, a young naval officer working under a false name,

78
00:04:43,472 --> 00:04:47,025
Takeo Yoshikawa, began sending regular reports.

79
00:04:47,025 --> 00:04:50,200
Washington decoded the request in October.

80
00:04:50,200 --> 00:04:52,922
Some officers called it a bomb plot.

81
00:04:52,922 --> 00:04:58,592
Most saw Japanese thoroughness, the same kind of reports Japan collected on

82
00:04:58,592 --> 00:05:00,708
ports from Panama to Manila.

83
00:05:00,708 --> 00:05:02,825
It was never sent to Hawaii.

84
00:05:03,425 --> 00:05:08,985
By November, the talks in Washington between Secretary of State Cordell Hull

85
00:05:08,985 --> 00:05:13,448
and the Japanese ambassador, Kichisaburo Nomura, had stalled.

86
00:05:13,448 --> 00:05:18,643
And thanks to Magic, Hull knew something Nomura could not say out loud.

87
00:05:18,643 --> 00:05:21,643
Tokyo had given its diplomats a deadline.

88
00:05:21,643 --> 00:05:23,618
First the 25th of November.

89
00:05:23,618 --> 00:05:26,472
Then, in a message decoded on the 22nd,

90
00:05:26,472 --> 00:05:28,667
a final extension to the 29th.

91
00:05:28,667 --> 00:05:33,495
After that, Tokyo wrote, things are automatically going to happen.

92
00:05:33,495 --> 00:05:36,934
Washington now knew that Japan had set a clock.

93
00:05:36,934 --> 00:05:41,324
It did not know what would happen when it ran out, or where.

94
00:05:41,324 --> 00:05:46,153
On the 25th of November, Roosevelt warned his war cabinet that the

95
00:05:46,153 --> 00:05:49,079
United States was likely to be attacked,

96
00:05:49,079 --> 00:05:52,006
perhaps as soon as the following Monday.

97
00:05:52,006 --> 00:05:55,225
Every eye in the room was on Southeast Asia.

98
00:05:55,825 --> 00:05:57,036
Twelve days left.

99
00:05:57,036 --> 00:06:01,810
On the 26th of November, Hull handed the Japanese a ten-point note.

100
00:06:01,810 --> 00:06:06,654
It demanded that Japan withdraw its forces from China and Indochina,

101
00:06:06,654 --> 00:06:10,288
terms Tokyo had already said it could never accept.

102
00:06:10,288 --> 00:06:14,420
That same day, in the foggy Kuril Islands far to the north

103
00:06:14,420 --> 00:06:18,553
of Japan, a fleet of six aircraft carriers weighed anchor.

104
00:06:18,553 --> 00:06:23,184
Two battleships, cruisers, destroyers and tankers went with them,

105
00:06:23,184 --> 00:06:25,962
more than four hundred aircraft in all.

106
00:06:25,962 --> 00:06:29,810
They took the empty northern route across the Pacific,

107
00:06:29,810 --> 00:06:33,871
where merchant ships rarely sailed, in rough winter seas,

108
00:06:33,871 --> 00:06:35,723
under total radio silence.

109
00:06:35,723 --> 00:06:40,212
Meanwhile, American scouts spotted a different Japanese convoy,

110
00:06:40,212 --> 00:06:43,489
heading south off Formosa, packed with troops.

111
00:06:43,489 --> 00:06:46,196
That was the fleet everyone could see.

112
00:06:46,196 --> 00:06:48,975
So that was the fleet everyone watched.

113
00:06:49,575 --> 00:06:50,840
Eleven days left.

114
00:06:50,840 --> 00:06:55,754
On the 27th of November, the Navy in Washington sent the strongest

115
00:06:55,754 --> 00:06:59,401
message in its history to Admiral Husband Kimmel,

116
00:06:59,401 --> 00:07:02,453
commander of the Pacific Fleet in Hawaii.

117
00:07:02,453 --> 00:07:06,771
It began: this dispatch is to be considered a war warning.

118
00:07:06,771 --> 00:07:11,684
An aggressive move by Japan was expected within the next few days.

119
00:07:11,684 --> 00:07:15,108
It listed the likely targets: the Philippines,

120
00:07:15,108 --> 00:07:18,681
Thailand, the Kra Peninsula, or possibly Borneo.

121
00:07:18,681 --> 00:07:20,691
Hawaii was not on the list.

122
00:07:20,691 --> 00:07:25,530
The Army sent its own message to Lieutenant General Walter Short,

123
00:07:25,530 --> 00:07:28,582
who commanded the defense of the islands.

124
00:07:28,582 --> 00:07:31,708
Hostile action was possible at any moment.

125
00:07:31,708 --> 00:07:35,654
If war came, Japan should commit the first overt act.

126
00:07:35,654 --> 00:07:39,450
And take care not to alarm the civilian population.

127
00:07:40,050 --> 00:07:45,286
General Short read that message and saw his biggest danger inside Hawaii,

128
00:07:45,286 --> 00:07:46,362
not out at sea.

129
00:07:46,362 --> 00:07:50,809
About a third of the islands' people were of Japanese descent,

130
00:07:50,809 --> 00:07:52,674
and Short feared sabotage.

131
00:07:52,674 --> 00:07:57,624
So he chose the lowest of his three alert levels, the sabotage alert.

132
00:07:57,624 --> 00:08:02,717
Aircraft were pulled out of their dispersal areas and parked wingtip to

133
00:08:02,717 --> 00:08:06,447
wingtip in neat rows, where guards could watch them.

134
00:08:06,447 --> 00:08:10,679
Ammunition was locked away, to keep it safe from saboteurs.

135
00:08:10,679 --> 00:08:16,991
Then Short cabled Washington: department alerted to prevent sabotage, liaison with Navy.

136
00:08:16,991 --> 00:08:19,501
In Washington, the reply was filed.

137
00:08:19,501 --> 00:08:23,016
Nobody wrote back to ask: sabotage, against what?

138
00:08:23,016 --> 00:08:26,244
The planes stayed in their rows for ten days,

139
00:08:26,244 --> 00:08:29,400
perfectly placed for an attack from the air.

140
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:35,424
Meanwhile, Admiral Kimmel used the war warning to reinforce the outer islands,

141
00:08:35,424 --> 00:08:37,232
where he expected trouble.

142
00:08:37,232 --> 00:08:42,308
On the 28th of November, the carrier Enterprise sailed to deliver fighter

143
00:08:42,308 --> 00:08:43,837
planes to Wake Island.

144
00:08:43,837 --> 00:08:48,705
On the 5th of December, the Lexington left to carry bombers to Midway.

145
00:08:48,705 --> 00:08:52,808
The third Pacific carrier, the Saratoga, was in California.

146
00:08:52,808 --> 00:08:55,589
The battleships, the pride of the fleet,

147
00:08:55,589 --> 00:08:59,483
stayed in harbor, tied up in pairs along Battleship Row,

148
00:08:59,483 --> 00:09:01,986
as they did on almost every weekend.

149
00:09:01,986 --> 00:09:05,115
No one ordered the carriers out to save them.

150
00:09:05,115 --> 00:09:08,940
Kimmel simply thought a carrier was more useful at sea.

151
00:09:08,940 --> 00:09:12,625
It was a lucky decision, and it would change the war.

152
00:09:13,225 --> 00:09:16,149
Quick question before the clock runs on.

153
00:09:16,149 --> 00:09:20,169
If you had been Admiral Kimmel on the 27th of November,

154
00:09:20,169 --> 00:09:24,555
holding a war warning that named every target except Hawaii,

155
00:09:24,555 --> 00:09:27,260
what would you have done differently?

156
00:09:27,260 --> 00:09:29,014
Tell me in the comments.

157
00:09:29,014 --> 00:09:32,450
I'll read the best answers in the next episode.

158
00:09:33,050 --> 00:09:34,037
Six days left.

159
00:09:34,037 --> 00:09:38,476
On the 1st of December, Japan's navy changed all its radio call

160
00:09:38,476 --> 00:09:41,154
signs, for the second time in a month.

161
00:09:41,154 --> 00:09:46,932
American radio intelligence in Hawaii, which tracked Japanese ships by their radio

162
00:09:46,932 --> 00:09:50,103
traffic, suddenly lost the aircraft carriers.

163
00:09:50,103 --> 00:09:53,063
On the 2nd, Kimmel's intelligence officer,

164
00:09:53,063 --> 00:09:58,136
Lieutenant Commander Edwin Layton, admitted he did not know where two of

165
00:09:58,136 --> 00:10:00,039
the carrier divisions were.

166
00:10:00,039 --> 00:10:04,408
Kimmel's reply became famous: do you mean to say they could be

167
00:10:04,408 --> 00:10:07,790
rounding Diamond Head, and you wouldn't know it?

168
00:10:07,790 --> 00:10:11,666
Layton said he hoped they would be sighted before then.

169
00:10:11,666 --> 00:10:15,823
That same day, the fleet at sea received a coded order from

170
00:10:15,823 --> 00:10:19,135
Tokyo: climb Mount Niitaka, one two zero eight.

171
00:10:19,135 --> 00:10:23,011
It meant: attack on the 8th of December, Japanese time.

172
00:10:23,011 --> 00:10:24,350
The 7th, in Hawaii.

173
00:10:24,950 --> 00:10:26,063
Five days left.

174
00:10:26,063 --> 00:10:28,364
On the 1st and 2nd of December,

175
00:10:28,364 --> 00:10:31,480
Tokyo ordered its embassies in Washington,

176
00:10:31,480 --> 00:10:38,308
London, Hong Kong, Singapore and Manila to destroy their code books and their code machines.

177
00:10:38,308 --> 00:10:39,940
Magic read every word.

178
00:10:39,940 --> 00:10:45,580
Destroying your codes is what an embassy does when it expects war, and soon.

179
00:10:45,580 --> 00:10:48,177
Admiral Kimmel was told on the 3rd.

180
00:10:48,177 --> 00:10:50,255
It did not change his alert.

181
00:10:50,255 --> 00:10:55,524
On the 2nd, Tokyo asked the Honolulu consulate for something new: daily

182
00:10:55,524 --> 00:10:58,492
reports on the warships in Pearl Harbor,

183
00:10:58,492 --> 00:11:03,761
and whether the base was protected by barrage balloons or torpedo nets.

184
00:11:03,761 --> 00:11:09,995
Barrage balloons and torpedo nets only matter for one thing: an attack from the air.

185
00:11:09,995 --> 00:11:12,444
That request was intercepted too.

186
00:11:12,444 --> 00:11:16,525
Washington did not translate it until after the attack.

187
00:11:17,125 --> 00:11:18,134
Two days left.

188
00:11:18,134 --> 00:11:22,530
On the 5th of December, the FBI recorded a strange phone call

189
00:11:22,530 --> 00:11:26,854
from a newspaper in Tokyo to a Japanese dentist in Honolulu.

190
00:11:26,854 --> 00:11:29,376
The caller asked about the weather,

191
00:11:29,376 --> 00:11:36,294
the searchlights, the aircraft overhead, and which flowers were in bloom: hibiscus, poinsettias.

192
00:11:36,294 --> 00:11:41,483
Army intelligence in Hawaii studied the transcript on the evening of the

193
00:11:41,483 --> 00:11:46,311
6th, and could not decide whether it was a code or just small talk.

194
00:11:46,311 --> 00:11:49,698
They agreed to look at it again in the morning.

195
00:11:49,698 --> 00:11:55,247
That same evening, the spy Yoshikawa sent his last report from the consulate.

196
00:11:55,247 --> 00:11:56,689
No barrage balloons.

197
00:11:56,689 --> 00:11:57,842
No torpedo nets.

198
00:11:57,842 --> 00:12:03,463
In his words, there was a considerable opportunity left for a surprise attack.

199
00:12:03,463 --> 00:12:06,129
It reached Washington's codebreakers.

200
00:12:06,129 --> 00:12:08,075
They decoded it on the 8th.

201
00:12:08,675 --> 00:12:09,773
The last night.

202
00:12:09,773 --> 00:12:14,753
On the 6th of December, Tokyo began sending Ambassador Nomura a long

203
00:12:14,753 --> 00:12:17,828
reply to the Hull note, in fourteen parts.

204
00:12:17,828 --> 00:12:21,050
By evening, thirteen parts had been decoded.

205
00:12:21,050 --> 00:12:23,833
At about half past nine in Washington,

206
00:12:23,833 --> 00:12:29,105
a young naval officer carried them to the White House in a locked pouch.

207
00:12:29,105 --> 00:12:31,521
Roosevelt read them in his study,

208
00:12:31,521 --> 00:12:36,354
then turned to his adviser Harry Hopkins and said: this means war.

209
00:12:36,354 --> 00:12:39,649
But the message named no target, and no time.

210
00:12:39,649 --> 00:12:42,945
The Navy's top officer, Admiral Harold Stark,

211
00:12:42,945 --> 00:12:45,288
was at the theater that evening,

212
00:12:45,288 --> 00:12:48,144
and no new warning went out that night.

213
00:12:48,144 --> 00:12:51,878
The Army's chief of staff, General George Marshall,

214
00:12:51,878 --> 00:12:53,562
was not reached at all.

215
00:12:53,562 --> 00:12:55,100
Nobody phoned Hawaii.

216
00:12:55,700 --> 00:12:57,382
The morning of the 7th.

217
00:12:57,382 --> 00:13:02,137
The fourteenth part arrived: Japan was breaking off negotiations.

218
00:13:02,137 --> 00:13:06,160
Then a short extra message, and this one was different.

219
00:13:06,160 --> 00:13:11,280
Ambassador Nomura was to deliver the full reply at exactly one o'clock

220
00:13:11,280 --> 00:13:13,767
in the afternoon, Washington time.

221
00:13:13,767 --> 00:13:17,571
An Army intelligence officer, Colonel Rufus Bratton,

222
00:13:17,571 --> 00:13:18,668
saw it at once.

223
00:13:18,668 --> 00:13:20,789
Why one o'clock, on a Sunday?

224
00:13:20,789 --> 00:13:25,983
One o'clock in Washington was half past seven in the morning in Hawaii.

225
00:13:25,983 --> 00:13:30,225
But General Marshall was out on his Sunday horseback ride,

226
00:13:30,225 --> 00:13:33,736
and did not reach his office until after eleven.

227
00:13:33,736 --> 00:13:37,906
He read all fourteen parts, then wrote a warning by hand.

228
00:13:37,906 --> 00:13:40,393
He chose not to use the telephone,

229
00:13:40,393 --> 00:13:42,660
which he feared was not secure.

230
00:13:42,660 --> 00:13:46,976
The Army's radio link to Hawaii was down because of static.

231
00:13:46,976 --> 00:13:51,365
So the most urgent warning of the war went out by commercial

232
00:13:51,365 --> 00:13:53,925
telegram, with no priority marking.

233
00:13:54,525 --> 00:13:59,331
In Hawaii, the warnings had already started arriving on their own.

234
00:13:59,331 --> 00:14:05,376
At 3:42 in the morning, a minesweeper spotted a periscope near the harbor entrance.

235
00:14:05,376 --> 00:14:10,182
At 6:45, the destroyer USS Ward found a small submarine trailing a

236
00:14:10,182 --> 00:14:13,095
cargo ship into the harbor, opened fire,

237
00:14:13,095 --> 00:14:17,100
hit its conning tower, and dropped depth charges on it.

238
00:14:17,100 --> 00:14:21,105
These were the first American shots of the Pacific War,

239
00:14:21,105 --> 00:14:23,727
more than an hour before the attack.

240
00:14:23,727 --> 00:14:25,766
The Ward radioed its report.

241
00:14:25,766 --> 00:14:29,698
But false submarine sightings were common around Oahu,

242
00:14:29,698 --> 00:14:33,485
and the report moved slowly up the chain of command,

243
00:14:33,485 --> 00:14:38,000
from one officer to another, each one asking for confirmation.

244
00:14:38,600 --> 00:14:43,251
At two minutes past seven, at the Opana radar station on Oahu's

245
00:14:43,251 --> 00:14:49,009
north shore, Privates Joseph Lockard and George Elliott were keeping their new

246
00:14:49,009 --> 00:14:53,808
radar running past its seven o'clock shutdown, just for practice.

247
00:14:53,808 --> 00:14:57,277
Then the blip appeared, 137 miles to the north.

248
00:14:57,277 --> 00:15:01,042
They called the information center at Fort Shafter.

249
00:15:01,042 --> 00:15:04,807
The only officer there was Lieutenant Kermit Tyler,

250
00:15:04,807 --> 00:15:08,720
a fighter pilot on only his second shift in the room,

251
00:15:08,720 --> 00:15:11,230
with no real training for the job.

252
00:15:11,230 --> 00:15:15,659
Tyler knew that a flight of American B-17 bombers was due in

253
00:15:15,659 --> 00:15:17,800
from California that morning.

254
00:15:17,800 --> 00:15:22,525
Hawaii's radio stations played music all night to guide them in.

255
00:15:22,525 --> 00:15:27,545
It was the same music the Japanese navigators tuned in to find Oahu.

256
00:15:27,545 --> 00:15:30,350
Tyler told them: don't worry about it.

257
00:15:30,950 --> 00:15:35,263
At 7:48, the first wave of Japanese aircraft reached Oahu,

258
00:15:35,263 --> 00:15:38,387
and minutes later the bombs began to fall.

259
00:15:38,387 --> 00:15:45,005
Over two waves, three hundred and fifty-three planes struck airfields and Battleship Row.

260
00:15:45,005 --> 00:15:50,583
Torpedoes, with their wooden fins, ran perfectly through the shallow water.

261
00:15:50,583 --> 00:15:54,673
A bomb set off the forward magazine of the USS Arizona,

262
00:15:54,673 --> 00:15:57,201
and 1,177 of her crew were killed.

263
00:15:57,201 --> 00:16:01,366
In under two hours, nineteen ships were sunk or damaged,

264
00:16:01,366 --> 00:16:07,687
including all eight battleships, and about one hundred and eighty-eight aircraft were

265
00:16:07,687 --> 00:16:11,405
destroyed, most of them parked wingtip to wingtip.

266
00:16:11,405 --> 00:16:15,793
2,403 Americans were killed, sixty-eight of them civilians.

267
00:16:15,793 --> 00:16:21,445
Japan lost twenty-nine aircraft, five midget submarines, and sixty-four men.

268
00:16:21,445 --> 00:16:27,096
In Washington, Japan's diplomats delivered their message at twenty past two,

269
00:16:27,096 --> 00:16:29,699
an hour after the bombs had fallen.

270
00:16:29,699 --> 00:16:32,079
And General Marshall's telegram?

271
00:16:32,079 --> 00:16:36,764
It reached General Short's headquarters hours after the attack.

272
00:16:36,764 --> 00:16:42,193
It was carried from the telegraph office by a young motorcycle messenger,

273
00:16:42,193 --> 00:16:44,275
himself of Japanese descent.

274
00:16:44,875 --> 00:16:52,069
Within weeks, a presidential commission blamed the two commanders in Hawaii for dereliction of duty.

275
00:16:52,069 --> 00:16:56,241
Kimmel and Short were relieved and retired at lower ranks,

276
00:16:56,241 --> 00:17:00,845
and spent the rest of their lives fighting to clear their names.

277
00:17:00,845 --> 00:17:05,593
Over the following years, there were nine official investigations.

278
00:17:05,593 --> 00:17:08,111
The final one, by Congress in 1946,

279
00:17:08,111 --> 00:17:11,204
spread the blame much wider: to Washington,

280
00:17:11,204 --> 00:17:15,736
which had read the warnings and failed to pass them on clearly.

281
00:17:15,736 --> 00:17:21,347
Some writers have claimed Roosevelt knew the target and let the attack happen.

282
00:17:21,347 --> 00:17:26,239
Historians have searched for decades, and found no evidence of that.

283
00:17:26,239 --> 00:17:29,620
The truth is less dramatic, and more troubling.

284
00:17:29,620 --> 00:17:36,022
In 1962 the historian Roberta Wohlstetter described it as a problem of signals and noise.

285
00:17:36,022 --> 00:17:37,605
Every warning existed.

286
00:17:37,605 --> 00:17:40,050
But they sat in different offices,

287
00:17:40,050 --> 00:17:44,726
under different secrets, surrounded by thousands of false alarms,

288
00:17:44,726 --> 00:17:48,179
and no single person ever held them all at once.

289
00:17:48,179 --> 00:17:50,266
That failure is why, in 1947,

290
00:17:50,266 --> 00:17:54,294
the United States created a Central Intelligence Agency,

291
00:17:54,294 --> 00:17:57,675
and a single system to put the pieces together.

292
00:17:58,275 --> 00:17:59,876
So that's Pearl Harbor.

293
00:17:59,876 --> 00:18:02,452
An embargo that put Japan on a clock,

294
00:18:02,452 --> 00:18:05,655
codebreakers who could read the clock ticking,

295
00:18:05,655 --> 00:18:08,857
a war warning that named every target but one,

296
00:18:08,857 --> 00:18:12,965
and a Sunday morning when the last signals were waved away.

297
00:18:12,965 --> 00:18:16,515
Japan won the morning, and lost the war it started.

298
00:18:16,515 --> 00:18:18,952
The carriers it missed were at sea,

299
00:18:18,952 --> 00:18:21,180
and six months later, at Midway,

300
00:18:21,180 --> 00:18:25,774
American carriers sank four of the six that attacked Pearl Harbor.

301
00:18:25,774 --> 00:18:30,578
Eighty-five years on, the question is still the one that haunts every

302
00:18:30,578 --> 00:18:34,825
intelligence service on Earth: what are we missing right now?

303
00:18:34,825 --> 00:18:37,261
Next time on Brinkman: another war,

304
00:18:37,261 --> 00:18:39,350
and the brink it started from.

